[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the phrase from your original request that still needs resolving: [1m"tasks they care about."[0m With one user, every
  task is theirs, so "care about" has to mean something narrower than ownership — otherwise it means "all of them."

  This decides whether notifications are opt-out (everything with a date nags you) or opt-in (silence until you ask),
  and it adds or removes a field on the task model, so it's worth settling before I write the design.

  - [1mEvery task with a due date[0m — no extra UI, no extra field; setting a date [3mis[0m the subscription. Simple and
    predictable, but every dated task nags, so users start omitting due dates to avoid noise — which corrupts the data.
  - [1mExplicit per-task reminder opt-in[0m — a "remind me" toggle (or a reminder time) per task. Notifications stay
    meaningful because the user chose each one. Costs one field and a bit of UI, and users may forget to opt in and then
    miss things.
  - [1mPriority/starred tasks only[0m — reuses a flag you'd probably want anyway, but conflates "important" with "remind me,"
    which aren't the same thing.

  I recommend the first: due date [3mis[0m the signal of caring. It's the least machinery, and the noise problem is better
  solved by tuning [3mwhen[0m alerts fire than by adding a second opt-in layer on top. We can add per-task control later if it
  actually grates — that upgrade is cheap, since it's one optional field.
